comments.body:
@ajayojha regarding the server-side enforcement point, the principle that security controls must be applied at a trusted service layer (and not rely on client-side enforcement) is a general one that applies across the whole standard. Rather than repeating it in individual requirements, I think we should add a note in the chapter paragraph text to make it clear that all encoding, sanitization, and other input handling controls are expected to be applied at a trusted service layer. This way the principle is visible without needing to state it in every requirement.

On the "well-known" wording, if @jmanico, @elarlang, and yourself really think it makes a difference, I can change this to "trusted" which is a more meaningful and less subjective term. The updated requirement would be:

"_Verify that all untrusted HTML input from WYSIWYG editors or similar is sanitized using a trusted and secure HTML sanitization library or framework feature._"

@tghosth  Good suggestion about placing the trusted service layer principle in the chapter paragraph.

I would like to share an additional practical perspective on how development teams may interpret this requirement.

On a practical level, there is a high chance that developers assume WYSIWYG editors already perform sufficient sanitization and may skip server-side enforcement. In practice, this is a common misunderstanding, especially when the editor library provides client-side sanitization. Even if the principle is defined at the chapter level, it would help if the requirement wording at least hinted that this control is expected to be enforced at the trusted service layer.
